        "Harold Y. Hwang",
        "Jochen Mannhart"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The discovery of conducting electron systems at oxide interfaces revealed an unexpected state of matter and opened a rich field of correlated-interface physics.",
        "attribution": "Ohtomo and Hwang’s interface experiments were pivotal; Mannhart’s independent and subsequent work helped establish the field. The record should separate the first observation from later confirmation.",
        "maturity": "The phenomenon has been intensively studied, though microscopic explanations and sample-dependent behavior remain active research topics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The roles of defects, intermixing, and electrostatic reconstruction in particular samples complicate a single account of the mechanism."
        ]
      }
    },
